<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Chat Template</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For our main experiments, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and adopt the LUFFY chat template across all training paradigms. This unified system prompt encourages systematic reasoning—analyze, summarize, explore, reassess, and refine—as illustrated in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Data Usage for Training ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We also evaluate with other popular templates shown in Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Data Usage for Training ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> to assess robustness under template shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data Usage for Training</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report training data usage for the 7B model in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (right), and we provide additional details for the 1.5B model in Figure <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Data Usage for Training ‣ Appendix D Implementation Details ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Because the 1.5B model has weaker reasoning capability, its lower rollout accuracy leads to a slight increase in SFT data consumption. Baseline data usage is compiled from the corresponding papers and from dataset statistics on Hugging Face. For the LUFFY baseline, we use the <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LUFFY}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>LUFFY</mtext><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LUFFY}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> variant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which has stronger reasoning performance, to reproduce results and record the associated data usage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04454v1/data-efficiency-1.5.svg" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/155;" width="274" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Data usage for training Qwen2.5-1.5B-Math model</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04454v1/chat-template.svg" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:494/465;" width="494" height="465"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Chat template details.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Experiments</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Hyperparameter Analysis</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method uses four hyperparameters: the rollout-accuracy threshold <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for buffering questions, the high-entropy token ratio <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the TopK freezing fraction <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and the history coefficient <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Figures <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ E.2 Template Ablation Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, these hyperparameters are insensitive and easy to tune. In Figure <a href="#A5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ E.2 Template Ablation Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> (left), the model performs best at <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\tfrac{2}{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\tfrac{2}{8}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since performance is comparable across nearby settings, we choose <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=\tfrac{1}{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=\tfrac{1}{8}</annotation></semantics></math> to reduce SFT data usage. Since the limit of computational resources, this analysis is conducted for only 1 epoch of training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Template Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In real-world deployment, users may not provide carefully designed chat prompts for LLM reasoning. It is therefore important to assess robustness in the wild. We evaluate MIFO under multiple reasoning templates to test whether it maintains strong performance without curated prompts. In Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Analyzing ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, we reported average reasoning accuracy and response length under the <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">No template</span> and <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> settings. Here we add results with another popular template, <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prime</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which is commonly used for RL-trained reasoning models, and we provide per-dataset scores in Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ E.2 Template Ablation Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ E.2 Template Ablation Study ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> further indicates that our method is template-robust, outperforming competitive baselines across all three templates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="A5.F10.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04454v1/hyperpara-buffer.svg" id="A5.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/548;" width="548" height="548"></object>
